{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"alpha blending in open gl alpha blending in open gl","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","fileName":"Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Alpha Blending in OpenGL","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-20 23:34\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[OpenGL Rendering Pipeline]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["OpenGL Rendering Pipeline","gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"alpha blending in open gl alpha blending in open gl","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","fileName":"Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Alpha Blending in OpenGL","heading":"Alpha Blending in OpenGL","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Alpha blending in OpenGL combines a source fragment with the color already stored in the framebuffer according to configured blend factors. A common rule weights the source by its alpha and the destination by one minus source alpha.\n\nBlending makes sprite transparency and translucent surfaces possible, but draw order matters because the operation is generally not commutative. Transparent geometry is usually rendered after opaque geometry and often requires back-to-front sorting.","tags":["baby"],"links":["OpenGL Rendering Pipeline","gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Alpha Blending in OpenGL.md","sourceType":"markdown","secti